welcome to this webinar titled making  sense out of multiplex  when and how to use the visio farm  phenotyping tools  today we're going to cover a number of  things at a very high level  firstly a brief introduction to what is  phenotyping  how it can be useful  and when people do it  i'll then touch on some manual  approaches that can be taken in order to  do multiplex phenotyping  next i'll introduce the visio farm  approach which is an easy unbiased one  and then lastly we'll touch on some of  the ways your data can be explored  we're going to start with an  introduction to multiplex phenotyping  and ask what is phenotyping itself  phenotyping is simply the ability to  characterize individual cells  based upon the presence or the absence  of particular markers  in this cartoon example i've taken three  markers green magenta and blue which you  can see on the schematics of cells on  the left hand side  and in this case you can see that the  presence or absence of these three  markers  indicates that a cell is either cell  type a where it's solely represented by  the green marker  cell type b which is present for both  the green and the blue marker and cell  type c which is present for magenta and  blue  an important concept here is that not  only is it relevant whether or not a  marker is present but also that a marker  is absent  cell type c for instance may be  classified as a different marker if it's  a triple positive  now this schematic example may seem  trivial but you can actually directly  relate  cell types a b and c to relevant  biological phenotypes in this case the  green marker is cd4 the blue is cd8 and  the magenta could be fox b3 in this case  we have three different biologically  relevant phenotypes of t helper cells  regulatory t cells and activated  cytotoxic t cells  the question becomes how do we even  classify cells as being positive or  negative for an individual marker  and in this case  what we do is to simply look within  individual channels of our low high or  ultraplex phenotyping data  this is one example from a fluorescence  experiment and we're just showing the  dappy or nuclear marker and fox p3 in  blue and yellow respectively  in this case the first step is to define  our phenotypic unit  by identifying the individual cells  you can see in this case that we've  identified all of the different cells  some of which are positive and some of  which are negative  we can then use a simple threshold  classifier in order to identify cells  that are positive for any individual  marker in this case the fox b3  and separate these cells or subset them  at this point for instance counting the  number of cells looking at the distance  apart from each other or looking at  other characteristics  now this is fairly trivial with a single  marker but what about the example we had  before in this case three different  markers the green magenta and pink and  blue from the first example  if we extend this concept of a manual  approach to phenotyping  we can look at the same example with the  three markers  and what we can do is to start to build  up a decision tree for these simple t  cell markers  in this case first looking for instance  at cd4 and deciding is that positive or  is it negative  if we look at one example a triple  positive in this case it's quite a rare  phenotype but you can take a cd4  positive  cd8 positive and fox p3 positive cell  and we can form an activated double  positive t cell  so this is very simple for one example  but it's important to realize that even  with three markers  the decision tree becomes quite  complicated  in this case for three markers we have  seven choices and that can produce a  maximum of eight different phenotypes  you can see those listed over here on  the right and this decision tree can be  walked through for each different marker  to identify positivity or negativity in  this case  so hopefully you can appreciate even for  three markers this is quite complicated  you can imagine 4 6 7 10 15 20 or 30  markers that are becoming more and more  commonplace these sorts of decision  trees become rather ungainly  and this leads me to highlight the  problems or at least two of the problems  with manual phenotyping or this  threshold based approach  first as i mentioned it's not scalable  beyond a few channels  even with six or seven channels it  becomes very difficult to build a  decision tree to identify each of the  different phenotypes  the second problem which is quite an  issue in realistic or real world data is  that it forces the user to pick a  threshold intensity to define positivity  so in this case the user would have to  go through however many channels you  have and identify whether or not a cell  is positive or negative and define a  cutoff  there are many problems with this  approach not least of all that in real  tissue the level of background intensity  often changes the level of  autofluorescence or the noise profile  may also change and so picking a single  threshold simply does not provide robust  results  what visio farm have done to address  this problem or both of these problems  is actually create a multiplex  phenotyping tool that's fully integrated  into the visio farm platform  and this is an automated streamlined and  unbiased approach to multiplex  phenotyping  we can take a practically unlimited  number of channels to support all  modalities  and these include some of the things  i've mentioned before like  immunofluorescence  and imaging mass cytometry all within  the same platform  one of the key points of the visiopharm  software is that it's actually  acquisition agnostic  so in this case for instance no matter  where the data come from  everything from what we would call  single or dual pass immunofluorescence  that can have three to eight channels  cyclic immunofluorescent techniques  where markers are stained on tissue  imaged and then stripped and restained  for different probes  or imaging mass cytometry techniques  which are doing above 30 markers at a  time and continuing to increase the  number  all of these different modalities  despite being different file formats and  coming from different systems can all be  opened and analyzed using the viziopharm  platform  so let's take a look at how multiplex  phenotyping works in visiofarm  as with other modules we build up  workflows based on modular apps or units  and so the first app would be to do a  cell detection or a detection of  phenotypic units  and in this case we lean on our advanced  ai deep learning tools in order to do a  robust cell detection even in complex  tissue  to take just a second we always use ai  deep learning tools to do cell detection  chiefly because it can cope with the  wide variants that we often see  this could be variance in cell size cell  shape or cell intensity  it also provides a more robust solution  to highly clustered environments where  cells can be much more easily separated  moving on to the multiplex once we've  detected a cell outlines  we then simply use our easy to use  interface here so that the user can  detect which channels they would like to  include or exclude  from their clustering analysis  very often we would want to exclude  structural markers or  obviously the nuclear marker that was  used as a cell outline  but sometimes functional markers may  also want to be excluded from this  phenotypic step although they can always  be accessed later  so the users select which channels  they'd like to exclude  from the list of single or multiple  channels here and then simply trains the  classifier to automatically find the  represented classes in your training  data  some of the parameters here are tunable  if desired and i'll mention this in the  next couple of slides  but what the classifier will produce is  a list of all the represented phenotypes  that are found in the the sample data  i'd like to just jump into a little more  detail about what happens when the user  pushes this train button  all of what i'm about to show you  happens in the background but it's  important to understand how much of a  time saver it is in terms of defining  the positivity and negativity of cells  within a population  for this demonstration i'm going to go  back to my original example of a  three channel cd4 cd8 fox p3  markers  and here you can see a combination of  cells in our samples that we're going to  use for training  so when the user provides these cells  for training  and then hits that train button  the first step is to assess the range of  intensity in each channel  and what this basically means is to  first start for instance with the cd4  channel  and look for each individual cell that's  used for training  what's the positivity in the cd4 channel  this allows the software to then build  up an intensity distribution and an  understanding of the range of  intensities within the cd4 channel  this is then repeated for a second  channel in this case the fox p3  and again the intensity distribution is  built up into a model  lastly we can do the same for our cd8  channel and again build up an intensity  distribution  the second step of training once we  understand or have built a probabilistic  model about the positivity and  negativity in our sample is to report  the represented combinations  so in this case we go back not only to  look at  individual channels but to understand  what's happening on the whole cell level  so in this case  these intensity distributions are used  to build a machine learning model  and then we take each of the individual  cells in the training data set and then  feed it into this model  so in this case we're taking a fox p3  and a cd8 positive cell  running it against our machine learning  model and it will decide using a  probabilistic approach that this cell is  positive for fox p3 and cd8  there's one other filtering step to  remove very niche classes or classes  that are not highly represented and  again this is user tunable  but assuming this filtering step is  passed then a class is defined as a cd8  fox p3 positive cell  this is repeated again for other cells  in the sampling data set and in this  case for instance this cell is passed  through the machine learning protocol  filtered  and then a new class which is cd4 alone  is added to the list  this goes on and on through the sampling  data set and then eventually this class  list is reported to the end user  this is a really beneficial approach  because it saves the user time during  development and also builds a more  robust robust model against deciding  which cells are positive for which  different markers  i will just make one more point when it  comes to the training  is that you may have noticed these boxes  in one of the previous slides  when we're defining positivity or  negativity sometimes if we have  particularly complex data with abnormal  noise profiles for instance or  multi-modal  intensity distributions  we may wish to  refine the data before it's added into  the model  and in this case what the user can do is  provide either value ranges or quantile  ranges into the classifier  what this does is to take that intensity  distribution and actually limit it to a  certain part this for instance can cut  off low band noise or reduce problems  with bleed through from different  channels  and at this point only these data that  lie within the thresholds or within the  quantiles are used for characterization  so what i've covered in the last couple  of slides is how we go about training a  classifier once this is done at the  beginning of an experiment what we end  up having is an app that can be run  against more data either single images  or batch processed across a whole  dataset  and in this case you can see an app up  in the ribbon  for multiplex phenotyping  in order to run this back across the  image we simply take an image that has  had the cell outlines identified the  same as before  run that app from the ribbon  and what we get is each one of the  individual cell masks classified into  one of the identified phenotypes  at this point you can see that all of  the masks are color coded based on their  phenotypes for easy identification  and as i mentioned this process once the  classifier is trained can be run as part  of a workflow so for instance downstream  of artifact exclusion or tumor  identification  and can be run as part of a batch  process as well scaling it up to many  hundreds of images  so i'd like to take the last part of  this this talk to talk about exploring  your data  once we have classified this individual  image to identify all the different  phenotypic classes within it and have  produced these overlay masks there are  lots of different things that we can do  with this data set  for instance  we can produce semi-quantitative or  qualitative heat maps  these are great for identifying hot  spots of particular phenotypes or  combinations of phenotypes  i say semi-quantitative because we can  also take these heat maps and use them  to define regions of interest for  instance identifying the hot spots of  cd68 macrophage markers t-cell markers  or any combination and then further  identifying or analyzing these areas  counting for instance other cell types  secondarily we have a wide range of  outputs and quantitative outputs  available in visiofarm so almost any  parameter can be measured and reported  on a per cell level  as a granular output to be able to  identify for instance cell size cell  shape the intensity distribution in that  particular class or even spatial  measurements such as the object distance  between any two classes for instance t  cells and macrophages or any classes and  any structure if you're interested in  looking at  immune cell infiltration or the distance  to blood vessels or the distance to  tumor boundaries  all of these can be reported at a very  granular level as well  i also want to mention that built into  the vizio farm software we can produce a  number of different phenographic outputs  i'm going to touch on these in the next  slide  the first is the phenotypic profile  and what this allows you to do is to  take the output of a multiplex  phenotyping analysis and then plot the  prevalence of each of the different  classes you see across multiple regions  of interest or multiple images  for instance if you're trying to discern  responders versus non-responders or  immune activation in different areas  this is a very nice way to quickly be  able to identify particular images or  particular rois that have a response  the second phenograph we can produce are  a number of different plots such as the  disney plot  in this case this clusters similar  objects as nearby points and dissimilar  objects as distant points  i'll also mention at this point that the  colors in the disney plot you see here  in the background are also directly  related to the colors in the overlays  that i showed in the last slide so it's  very easy to take a particular class and  identify the cell types back in the  original image  not only can we produce the type plot as  you see in the background but the per  channel heat maps as well to identify  the contributions of particular types of  cells  another plot we can create is what we  call the phenotypic matrix  in this case this is very useful for  probing the composition of individual  phenotypic classes  a good example for instance is if you  have activated t cells that are positive  for cd8 and then granzyme b  to make them cytotoxic  in this case we may want to see in  different conditions  which cells are more positive or which  images show more positivity in the  granzyme b class than other ones again  the matrix allows you to probe these  sorts of questions  the last phenotypic plot i'd like to  mention today is the neighborhood  analysis  and what this allows you to do again  very easily at the push of a button is  to run a statistical analysis or a  statistical spatial analysis  to indicate whether or not the  interactions between two different  phenotypes are pretty much by chance or  if they're more likely than you would  expect if all of the different cells  were distributed randomly  this is great for identifying particular  cell populations and whether or not  they're associating with each other or  being actively excluding from each other  very important in things like antigen  presenting cells that rely on cell cell  interactions  as i briefly mentioned all of these  phenographs are available from the  ribbon at the top of the viziofarm  software it requires no coding or no  scripting in order to produce any of  these plots or run any of the analysis  i've spoken about today so it's a really  easy way for new users to be able to do  quite complex exploratory analysis  without much training and without much  need to to get to know new features of  the software  the other important key with the vizio  farm software is that your data are  always accessible to you  so for instance if you run a clustering  analysis or a phenotypic analysis you  can always export any of the data the  cell positions the intensities the  classification size shape texture  parameters all of these data can be  easily exported for single images or  whole data sets into simple text files  such as tab separated value lists  but we can also export the overlays that  are produced so for instance if you  wanted to do a custom visualization we  can always export the overlays as tiff  files or if you prefer vectors xml  just to summarize briefly what we've  covered today  the visiopharm multiplex phenotyping is  an easy to use easy to configure  unbiased multiplex clustering tool  we've taken incredible complexity here  and made it very simple to use and no  step do you require any programming  learning new syntactic languages or  coding  we're also format agnostic so everything  i've shown you today can be applied to  almost any data type be it imaging mass  cytometry fluorescence or other  techniques  importantly multiplex phenotyping  invisiofarm integrates with existing  tools this means you can leverage the  power of our ai deep learning both for  cell detection but also as i briefly  mentioned identifying areas of interest  excluding artifacts or necrotic areas or  any other upstream tool that could  benefit from ai deep learning  and lastly the outputs are fully  customizable you really do get to decide  what questions you ask of your data  this could be quantitative measures as i  mentioned as summary statistics or  granular per cell data  density heat maps are very easy to  produce and to use  or are built built-in phenotyping plots  including disney's and neighborhood  analysis  i'd like to thank you for watching this  webinar today  i hope you've gained some valuable  insights into multiplexing and how the  vizio farm platform approaches these  sorts of analyses

